<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hispania Baetica</h1><p class="st-meta">27 BCE – 411 CE (approx.) · capital Corduba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The wealthy southern Roman province of the Guadalquivir valley, homeland of the emperors Trajan and Hadrian and of Seneca.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Baetica was created from the southern part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> by Augustus from 27 BCE, named after the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a>, the Guadalquivir. It covered the former lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turdetani">Turdetani</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bastetani">Bastetani</a> and the old Phoenician cities, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Corduba</a> as its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As a peaceful, heavily urbanised region, Baetica was a public province governed by a proconsul chosen by the Senate. It exported vast amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-testaccio">Monte Testaccio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, as well as fish sauce, wine and metals. Its elite rose high: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca</a> and Lucan came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Corduba</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the invasions of 409 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silingi">Siling Vandals</a> received Baetica in 411. They were crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Hasding Vandals</a> passed through to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 429. Later fought over by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> and Visigoths, the region became the heartland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hispania-baetica.jpg" alt="Hispania Baetica" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Baetica_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>27 BCE – 411 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corduba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Baetica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=192&amp;focus=hispania-baetica" aria-label="Hispania Baetica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4561 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hispania Baetica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4881 1072L4881 1072M4881 1072L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1072h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corduba. <a href="/atlas?year=192&amp;focus=hispania-baetica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
